<PaperLayout
  title="MERLIN"
  subtitle="Multimodal Embedding Refinement via LLM-based Iterative Navigation for Text-Video Retrieval-Rerank"
  venue="EMNLP 2024 · Industry Track"
  authors={authors}
  links={links}
  description="MERLIN refines text-video query embeddings through training-free LLM-based iterative feedback, aligning short user queries with the actual videos they have in mind."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Abstract</h2>
    <p>
      Users typically issue short, under-specified queries; videos contain rich
      multimodal context. This mismatch between query and content is a primary
      driver of retrieval errors, and one that prior work — focused on cross-modal
      interaction, foundation-model scaling, or probabilistic modeling — largely
      ignores. We introduce <strong>MERLIN</strong> (Multimodal Embedding Refinement
      via LLM-based Iterative Navigation): a training-free pipeline that uses an
      LLM and a multimodal LLM to simulate clarifying interactions, gradually
      refining the query embedding toward the user's intent. On MSR-VTT, MSVD, and
      ActivityNet, MERLIN delivers substantial gains in Recall@1 over strong
      retrieval baselines.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The query–video gap</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src="/merlin/img/fig1.png" alt="Illustration of the discrepancy between short user queries and rich video content" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>A short user query rarely captures the full context of the target video. MERLIN closes this gap iteratively, without retraining the underlying retrieval model.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Pipeline</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src="/merlin/img/fig2.png" alt="MERLIN architecture: question generation, video QA, aggregation, embedding refinement" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>MERLIN runs over an off-the-shelf retriever. At each round, an LLM asks a clarifying question grounded in the top-1 video; a multimodal LLM answers from sampled frames; the answer embedding is fused into the query via SLERP.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Question generation.</strong> An LLM uses the top-ranked video's
        metadata to generate a clarifying question — eliciting context the user
        didn't initially provide.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Human-simulating agent.</strong> A multimodal LLM answers the
        question by analyzing frames sampled at 1-second intervals; an aggregation
        step fuses frame-level answers into a video-level response.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Iterative embedding refinement.</strong> The current answer
        embedding is combined with the running query embedding via SLERP
        (spherical linear interpolation) with α=0.8 — biasing toward earlier
        embeddings to prevent query drift.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Main results</h2>
    <p>Recall@1 / Recall@5 / Recall@10 from round 0 (baseline retriever) to round 5 (after five MERLIN iterations).</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="results">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Dataset</th>
            <th>R@1 (R0 → R5)</th>
            <th>R@5 (R0 → R5)</th>
            <th>R@10 (R0 → R5)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="method">MSR-VTT</td>
            <td>44.40 → <strong>78.00</strong></td>
            <td>67.60 → 94.20</td>
            <td>76.20 → 96.80</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="method">MSVD</td>
            <td>52.39 → <strong>77.61</strong></td>
            <td>77.16 → 94.48</td>
            <td>84.78 → 97.31</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="method">ActivityNet</td>
            <td>56.58 → <strong>68.44</strong></td>
            <td>84.77 → 91.95</td>
            <td>91.73 → 96.63</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="table-caption">+76% relative R@1 on MSR-VTT, +48% on MSVD, +21% on ActivityNet — without training a new model.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why iteration helps — and how it can hurt</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src="/merlin/img/fig4.png" alt="Average rank improvement across QA rounds" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Average rank of the target video improves monotonically across rounds for α = 0.8.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The fusion coefficient <em>α</em> controls how much weight the running
      embedding keeps versus the new round's answer. At <strong>α = 0.8</strong>,
      gains accumulate stably across rounds. At <strong>α = 0.2</strong>, the
      query is dragged by each new answer — initial rounds help, then performance
      <em>declines</em> as the embedding drifts away from the user's original
      intent.
    </p>
  </section>

